-- title:
--   Places above an unramified place as Frobenius orbits on cosets
-- statement:
--   Let $E$ and $K$ be number fields with $K$ a Galois extension of $E$, write $G = K \simeq_{\mathrm{alg}[E]} K$ for its Galois group, and let $F$ be an intermediate field of $K/E$. Let $v$ be a height-one prime of the ring of integers $\mathcal{O}_E$, let $Q$ be a maximal ideal of $\mathcal{O}_K$ whose contraction to $\mathcal{O}_E$ is the ideal underlying $v$, assume that the inertia subgroup of $Q$ inside $G$ is trivial, and let $\sigma \in G$ be an arithmetic Frobenius at $Q$ over $\mathcal{O}_E$ in the sense of `IsArithFrobAt`. Then there is a bijection $e$ between the set of height-one primes $u$ of $\mathcal{O}_F$ whose contraction to $\mathcal{O}_E$ is $v$ and the set of orbits of the cyclic subgroup `Subgroup.zpowers σ` generated by $\sigma$ acting on the coset space $G / F.\mathrm{fixingSubgroup}$, where $F.\mathrm{fixingSubgroup}$ is the subgroup of elements of $G$ fixing $F$ pointwise, with the property that for every such $u$ the inertia degree `inertiaDeg'` of the ideal underlying $v$ in the ideal underlying $u$ equals the cardinality of the orbit $e(u)$.
--
--   This is Dedekind's description of the splitting of an unramified place of $E$ in a not necessarily normal intermediate field $F$: the places of $F$ above $v$ correspond to the orbits of the Frobenius (equivalently, the decomposition group, here cyclic since inertia is trivial) on the coset space $G/\mathrm{Gal}(K/F)$, the residue degree of a place being the length of the corresponding orbit. It is used in the base-change computations for quaternionic data in the Langlands–Tunnell input, via [`LanglandsTunnell.formalBaseChange_quatH_a_eq_of_orderOf_eq_eight`](thm.html#LanglandsTunnell.formalBaseChange_quatH_a_eq_of_orderOf_eq_eight).
